A||||6||Vader||This could happen to you...||||1||
Z||000000||Vader||05-28-2001||11:39 PM||||My Fiero last July:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/Fiero01.jpg"> <P>My Fiero last August:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/FieroCrash05.jpg"> <P>My Fiero yesterday:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/Fiero06.jpg"> <P>Last May I had enlarged the valves, ported the head, ported the intake and matched all the openings, deleted the stupid little plate under the TB, reflowed the injector, installed a Comp Cam, and modded the FPR to an adjustable unit.  I suspected I would have a fuel map problem but it ran well until it was hit while parked.  <P>Since last October when repairs were nearly completed, the car has not run well.  I suspected a fuel delivery problem, but the pump would run and idle was notably rich-smelling.  <P>I adjusted fuel pressure multiple times, retimed, swapped the ECM for fear of a marginal connection due to impact, replaced the O2 sensor (again) then installed a "slow" O2 to fool the ECM a little rich, checked all other sensors for correct range, changed the MAP, bypassed the EGR for testing, changed fuel filters twice fearing loose debris from the impact, and pulled my hair out.  <P>Today I got serious and used a fabricated test adapter to check fuel pressure again.  The pump would produce about 6½ PSIG either idling or dead-headed (return line blocked).  The pressure would drop immediately on shut-down.  I bit the bullet and dropped the tank just to check (after getting a new AC pump).  What I found was very interesting.  Those of you who have been here will recognize the problem here and see why the pressure was "funky" and would read normal sometimes in the past.  This is EXACTLY the way the unit came out of the top of the tank:<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/Fieropump.jpg"> <P>I just thought I'd share that in case anyone else ever has a mysteriously varying fuel pressure problem.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||05-29-2001||12:49 AM||||Somebody forgot the clamps!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Vader||05-29-2001||12:53 AM||||It must have been the factory, because the hose used in place of the pulsation damper never showed signs of any clamps.  The new one is securely clamped, however.  I was a bit surprised by that as well, and was even more impressed that the fuel pressure would actually be correct at times.  The return line was bent just enough to open the connection - I wish it would have bent just a little more several onths ago...<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||11:24 AM||||I have no idea what that photo is.<P> the silver can on the bottom is the fuel pump?<P>  the black hose half way up on the left should be clamped at both end?<P> the fuel sender float is on the right side?<P>  sounds like all you needed is a couple clamps. did you put it back in and return the new few pump, or replace it anyway since it was out?||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||05-29-2001||01:41 PM||||There are no clamps unless someone adds them. Usually the replacement kit comes with zip-tie style clamps to make you feel good putting it together. The fuel pump just wedges into the tank unit. Your crash caused the bottom bracket that holds the fuel pump up to the feed tube to bend. When the pump dropped far enough the tube pulled loose.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mblackwell||05-29-2001||04:00 PM||||When I went to replace my pump, mine was totally disconnected.  Turned out to be dead anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||trigger||05-29-2001||09:03 PM||||You know I saw everything in that picture two weeks ago.  Hmmm that's strange.  Mine wasn't bent though, it was letting air in the lines.  Wouldn't run right after a good heat soak.<P>Vader:  What ground f/x are on your car?<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Trigger.gif"> ||||reg||1
